<spell-notebook "flyout usage">
	<header level="1">
		Flyout
		<header>A flyout is a modal that slides in from a side of the page.</>
	</>
	<flyout-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#opening-it">Opening it</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  what a flyout is, opening, dismissal, events, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A flyout is `<ui-modal>`'s element in a flyout's clothes: the same `<dialog>` opened with `showModal()`, so
			focus moves inside, the rest of the page is inert and scroll-locked, **Escape** closes only the topmost overlay,
			and focus returns to whatever opened it. Its dimmer is lighter than a modal's. Loading the flyout family
			defines `<ui-modal>` too.

			Write a `<ui-flyout>` anywhere in the page with its parts inside -- `<ui-header>`, `<ui-content>`,
			`<ui-actions>` with buttons -- or the `header` / `content` shorthands. There is no `.flyout()` call to make.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Opening it">
		<markdown>
			- With no script, from a native `<button commandfor="id" command="--show">` or a `<ui-button>` (an invoker
			  command; Fomantic's `attach events`). `--close` closes it and `--toggle` flips it (Fomantic's `toggle`).
			- From code, write `open` (which fires no `ui-opening` / `ui-closing`).

			`closedby` says what dismisses it: `any` (the default: **Escape** or a click on the dimmer), `closerequest`
			(**Escape** only) or `none` (only its own buttons). `closable` adds the close icon; `closable="false"` is also
			Fomantic's `closable: false`, unless `closedby` is set.
		</>

		<section label="Toggle from a menu" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example "Toggle from a menu" description="A menu item drives the flyout through a button with `command=\"--toggle\"`.">
				<menu attach="top">
					<item>
						<button commandfor="flyout-usage-menu" command="--toggle" icon="sidebar" appearance="basic">Toggle flyout</button>
					</item>
				</menu>
				<segment attach="bottom">
					<header level="3">Application Content</header>
					<image src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/short-paragraph.png" alt=""/>
				</segment>
				<flyout id="flyout-usage-menu" closable>
					<header>
						<icon name="question"/>
						<content>Archive Old Messages</content>
					</header>
					<content>
						<text paragraph>Your inbox is getting full, would you like us to enable automatic archiving of old messages?</text>
					</content>
					<actions>
						<button class="cancel" color="red" icon="remove">No</button>
						<button class="ok" color="green" icon="checkmark">Yes</button>
					</actions>
				</flyout>
			</example>
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported">Not ported: a custom <text code>context</> (a flyout inside a segment instead of the page) -- the flyout is a modal <text code>&lt;dialog&gt;</> in the top layer, always against the viewport. For a panel inside a region, use <text code>&lt;ui-sidebar&gt;</> in a <text code>&lt;ui-pushable&gt;</>.</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			Buttons inside act by Fomantic's classes: `approve` / `ok` / `positive` approve, `deny` / `cancel` / `negative`
			deny, each first dispatching the cancelable `ui-approving` / `ui-denying` (`{ action, originalEvent? }`).

			`visible` is the controlled state, as on every element (`hidden` is its other name): writing either shows
			or hides the flyout and fires nothing. A flyout starts hidden.

			- `ui-opening` (cancelable) -- a user asked to open it, through an invoker command; `detail` is
			  `{ visible: true, originalEvent? }`.
			- `ui-closing` (cancelable) -- about to close; `detail` is `{ visible: false, reason, originalEvent? }`, and
			  `reason` is `escape`, `outside`, `close`, `approve`, `deny` or `close-all`.
			- `ui-opened` / `ui-closed` -- shown or hidden, once the slide has finished (Fomantic's `onVisible` / `onHidden`).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const help = document.querySelector("#help")
			help.addEventListener("ui-approving", (event) => {
			  if (!form.checkValidity()) event.preventDefault() // stay shown
			})
			help.addEventListener("ui-closing", (event) => console.log(event.detail.reason))
			help.visible = true // show from code:  no ui-opening
			```
		</>
		<message size="small" class="site-not-ported">Not ported: Fomantic's behaviors (show, hide, toggle ...), its dimPage, returnScroll and transition settings, and its callbacks. Instead, write <text code>visible</>, use invoker commands, and listen for the events.</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- **Tab** / **Shift+Tab** move between the controls inside the flyout, never out.
			- **Enter** / **Space** activate the focused button (approve, deny, close).
			- **Escape** closes it (`closedby` `any` or `closerequest`).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A real modal `<dialog>`: focus moves inside and **Tab** stays there, the rest of the page is inert, and focus
			  returns to the opener when it closes.
			- Named by the host's `aria-label`, else the `header` shorthand, else a slotted `<ui-header>`. A flyout with
			  neither (a cookie bar) needs `aria-label`.
			- The close icon is a real button named "Close" (translated), placed last so the first focus lands in the
			  content. Put `autofocus` on the control that should take focus first.
			- **Escape** closes it unless `closedby="none"`; with `none`, make sure a button inside closes it.
			- If the element fails to render, there's no dialog: it shows its children through a bare `<slot>`, in
			  the page where they're written rather than over it, so nothing traps focus, dims the page or closes
			  on Escape. `open`, invoker commands, the `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` vetoes and the slide go.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `open` to your state and listen for `ui-closing` (and `ui-approving` / `ui-denying`) to follow the user.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-flyout visible={open} position="right" heading="Help" onui-close={() => setOpen(false)}>
			  ...
			</ui-flyout>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-flyout :visible="open" position="right" heading="Help" @ui-closing="open = false">...</ui-flyout>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-flyout
			  visible={open()}
			  position="right"
			  heading="Help"
			  on-ui-closing={() => setOpen(false)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-flyout>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
